-- title:
--   Euler characteristic of twists by n degree-one points
-- statement:
--   Let $k$ be a field, let $Y$ be a scheme with a proper morphism $y : Y \to \operatorname{Spec} k$, and let $\mathcal{W}$ be a two-affine open cover of $Y$, that is, two affine opens $U_0, U_1$ with $U_0 \sqcup U_1 = \top$ and $U_0 \sqcap U_1$ affine. Let $n \in \mathbb{N}$ and let $\mathfrak{p} : \mathrm{Fin}\,n \to Y.\mathrm{IdealSheafData}$ be ideal sheaves, each invertible in the sense that around every point of $Y$ there is an affine basic open on which the ideal is generated by a single non-zero-divisor; assume moreover that for each $i$ the composite of the closed immersion of the subscheme cut out by $\mathfrak{p}_i$ with $y$ is finite and has `finrank` equal to $1$ at every point of $\operatorname{Spec} k$. Let $L$ be an $\mathcal{O}_Y$-module that is invertible, i.e. trivialised by the unit module on an open neighbourhood of each point, and let $d \in \mathbb{N}$. Write $F$ for the right-nested tensor product $\mathfrak{p}_0.\mathrm{module} \otimes (\cdots \otimes \mathbf{1})$ over the list `List.finRange n`, where $I.\mathrm{module}$ is the kernel of the map from the unit module to the pushforward of the unit along the closed immersion of the subscheme of $I$, and $F'$ for the analogous product formed with $I.\mathrm{invModule} =$ the dual of $I.\mathrm{module}$. For an $\mathcal{O}_Y$-module $M$ let $\chi(M) = \dim_k H^0 - \dim_k H^1$ of the two-chart Čech complex $\mathcal{W}.\mathrm{sectionsOf}\,y\,M$ given by sections over $U_0$, $U_1$ and $U_0 \sqcap U_1$, with $H^0$ the kernel and $H^1$ the cokernel of the difference of the two restrictions. The conclusion is the conjunction of $\chi(L \otimes F^{\otimes d}) = \chi(L) - d\,n$ and $\chi(L \otimes F'^{\otimes d}) = \chi(L) + d\,n$, as identities in $\mathbb{Z}$.
--
--   This is the Riemann–Roch bookkeeping for twisting an invertible module by $n$ points of degree one over the base field, computed through the Čech complex of a two-chart affine cover rather than through abstract cohomology. It feeds the degree computations for the two-chart model of the modular curve $X_1(p)$, where the points in question are the crossing points of a component.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/anthropics/fermats-last-theorem/blob/aa2d8b34692b16c70f699536de0d8e75b9a3e9ef/Theorems/Thm_AlgebraicGeometry_Scheme_IdealSheafData_IsInvertible_eulerChar_sectionsOf_tensor_tensorPow_foldr_module_eq_sub_and_invModule_eq_add_of_finrank_eq_one.lean

